# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# doctor-web — environment variables
#
# This file lists EVERY variable the app reads. There are three, and all three
# are required: `requireEnv` throws at the point of use rather than falling back,
# so a missing value fails loudly instead of producing a client that silently
# talks to nowhere.
#
# The earlier version of this file carried keys for patient intake, the LLM
# providers, CLOVA Speech, PubMed and the public-data (공공데이터) integrations.
# None of that code is in this app any more — intake belongs to `kiosk/`, and the
# consultation/analysis features belong to the Electron app in `src/`. Keys for
# code that no longer exists are worse than no documentation, because somebody
# will provision them.
#
# Verify this list before adding to it:
#   grep -rn "requireEnv\|process.env" src/
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase — project `yhwvwojjwwlcrvpfxgag` (`realtime-doctor`, ap-northeast-2)
#
# This is the LIVE production database of the native RightHand app. The web
# dashboard is a read-only consumer of it: the four `f_web_stats_*` functions
# added by `supabase/migrations/0014_web_statistics.sql` do all the aggregation
# on the database side.
#
# The web's own former Supabase project (`iqrkfqtifjanseoknkpq`) has been
# deleted. If you find that id anywhere else, it is stale.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Server-side only. Bypasses RLS — never expose to the browser.
#
# Used for exactly one write: the `web_stats_export_audit` row recorded when a
# doctor exports the statistics CSV (`src/lib/audit.ts`). That table grants
# nothing to `authenticated` on purpose, so the service role is the only way to
# write it — an audit row the audited party can suppress or forge is not an
# audit row.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Observability (O1) — the prober at /api/ops/probe
#
# The header comment at the top of this file used to say there were exactly
# three variables. There are now five, and the two below are NOT interchangeable
# in how they fail:
#
#   * CRON_SECRET missing  -> the prober REFUSES to run and returns 503. Nothing
#     is silently skipped; /api/health then reports the prober as never having
#     run.
#   * OPS_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL missing -> the prober still runs and still records
#     everything, but alerts are DELIVERED NOWHERE. That state is reported on
#     every run (ops_probe_runs.alert_target_configured = false, alert_error
#     set, the undelivered messages stored verbatim in details.wouldHaveSent)
#     and by the `alerting` check in /api/health.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Shared secret for the scheduled prober.
#
# [HARD] The name matters. Vercel Cron attaches `Authorization: Bearer
# $CRON_SECRET` to its requests ONLY when the project has an environment
# variable with exactly this name. Rename it and the cron gets 401 on every run
# and never succeeds, without anything appearing to be wrong.
#   Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
CRON_SECRET=

# Outbound alert target. Optional today, and the system is explicit about that.
#
# Any endpoint that accepts a JSON POST works — Slack/Discord incoming webhooks,
# Google Chat, ntfy, n8n, or a personal endpoint. The payload carries both a
# `text` field (what chat apps render) and structured fields (surface, issue,
# severity, detail, runId). Deliberately one URL and no per-channel SDK: the
# channel has not been chosen yet, and choosing it should not be a code change.
# OPS_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Observability tuning — all optional, all with working defaults
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# How often the prober is expected to run, in minutes. Must match the schedule
# in vercel.json: the value is written to ops_probe_runs.expected_next_run_at
# and every staleness reader compares against it. Default 1440 (daily), which is
# the ceiling on Vercel's Hobby plan, not a design preference. See
# src/lib/ops/schedule.ts.
# OPS_PROBE_INTERVAL_MINUTES=1440

# How long before the same (surface, issue) is alerted again. Default 1440.
# OPS_ALERT_REPEAT_MINUTES=1440

# Consecutive failing runs required before alerting. Default 1 (alert at once).
# Raise to 2 to ignore single flaps.
# OPS_ALERT_STREAK=1

# Public origin the prober probes. Default https://entanglecare.com — the path
# real users take, so DNS, TLS and the kiosk rewrite are inside the check.
# OPS_PROBE_ORIGIN=https://entanglecare.com
